WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:09.274
This is Chapter 2: ownership
and the orphan rule.

4
00:00:09.274 --> 00:00:14.134
An account nobody will own is the one
nobody will notice when it's misused, so

5
00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:19.150
the rule for it is decided once, written
down, and run by code a reviewer has read.

6
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.537
We'll start from Learn's own Graph
samples for finding ownerless apps,

7
00:00:23.537 --> 00:00:27.617
see what they miss, then walk the
ownership check, the orphan rule

8
00:00:27.617 --> 00:00:31.850
as a reviewed plan, the separate
write path, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:37.360
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.672
Why does ownership come
before everything else?

11
00:00:43.672 --> 00:00:46.435
Because an owner isn't
just a name on a form.

12
00:00:46.435 --> 00:00:49.738
An owner can add a credential
to an app and act as it,

13
00:00:49.738 --> 00:00:52.960
so an app nobody owns is
an app nobody is watching.

14
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.884
The plan's second gate asks that
every non-person identity be owned,

15
00:00:56.884 --> 00:01:00.380
and that the ones nobody claims
are disabled, never deleted,

16
00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.448
with a thirty-day window
for someone to step forward.

17
00:01:03.448 --> 00:01:07.728
Learn points the same way: at least
two owners, a reference to a team

18
00:01:07.728 --> 00:01:12.850
record that outlives any one person,
and a short list reviewed regularly.

19
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.500
Which cloud?

20
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.202
For this estate, a GCC tenant,

21
00:01:19.202 --> 00:01:24.307
every Graph read and write goes to the
worldwide endpoint, and the three Graph calls

22
00:01:24.307 --> 00:01:29.338
this chapter writes with are all listed
for the government service levels as well.

23
00:01:29.338 --> 00:01:33.680
The code takes the host from its cloud
table and checks it on every request,

24
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.237
which matters because Learn's tutorial
simply hard-codes the worldwide host.

25
00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:43.341
On the directory side, the disables go only
to the domain you name, and one detail

26
00:01:43.341 --> 00:01:48.960
to remember: the disable command won't work
against a read-only domain controller.

27
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:53.772
So what's the rule?

28
00:01:53.772 --> 00:01:56.433
Learn is permissive: a service principal's

29
00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:59.473
owners can be users or
other service principals.

30
00:01:59.473 --> 00:02:03.322
The program is stricter, and
this is labeled design guidance.

31
00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:08.672
Every app needs two human owners who are
enabled members, not guests and not bots.

32
00:02:08.672 --> 00:02:11.555
Its reference has to
point at a real record in

33
00:02:11.555 --> 00:02:14.360
the system inventory,
not a line of free text.

34
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.860
And every directory service
account needs an application owner.

35
00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:21.542
Active Directory has no owners
attribute the code can trust,

36
00:02:21.542 --> 00:02:25.000
so that owner comes from a
file kept with the record.

37
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.450
Now the orphan rule itself.

38
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.696
The plan's decision, owned by the
chief information security officer,

39
00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:38.868
is to disable first, once the
identity governance officer confirms,

40
00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:41.550
then give the office
thirty days to claim it.

41
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.440
Book 04 runs the other way round,

42
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.800
and the chapter keeps that difference
open rather than quietly picking.

43
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.150
Learn agrees on the shape:
disable, don't delete.

44
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.675
It offers two reversible switches.

45
00:02:53.675 --> 00:02:56.612
One deactivates the
registration everywhere,

46
00:02:56.612 --> 00:02:59.550
but its procedure is still
on the beta endpoint.

47
00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.457
The other disables sign-in in this
tenant on the stable endpoint,

48
00:03:03.457 --> 00:03:05.650
and that's the one the code uses.

49
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.550
Microsoft's starting point
here is a Graph tutorial.

50
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.531
It gives a query for service
principals with no owner or just one,

51
00:03:16.531 --> 00:03:20.962
requests that add an owner, and a
sample that sets the service reference.

52
00:03:20.962 --> 00:03:26.099
As before, those samples exist only
inside the page, so the repository pins

53
00:03:26.099 --> 00:03:31.488
its source unchanged, with hashes a test
checks and a weekly watch for changes.

54
00:03:31.488 --> 00:03:36.304
The harness then reads each request straight
out of the page and sends it to a fake

55
00:03:36.304 --> 00:03:38.533
Graph, which behaves like the real one,

56
00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:43.420
right down to refusing an advanced query
that's missing its required header.

57
00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.507
Sending the samples to the fake
shows the problem quickly.

58
00:03:50.507 --> 00:03:53.646
The query's shape is right,
so it's kept as the model.

59
00:03:53.646 --> 00:03:58.091
But most of what it calls ownerless
is noise: managed identities,

60
00:03:58.091 --> 00:04:02.370
which have no owners to add, a
third party's app, and Graph itself.

61
00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:05.564
It checks the service principal,
when the owners who matter

62
00:04:05.564 --> 00:04:08.758
are the ones on the registration,
where the credentials live.

63
00:04:08.758 --> 00:04:12.295
It counts a guest or a bot as
an owner, just like a person.

64
00:04:12.295 --> 00:04:15.132
And it labels a simple read
with a write permission.

65
00:04:15.132 --> 00:04:17.610
Each of these is pinned by a test.

66
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.710
Then there's what the
samples don't cover at all.

67
00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.947
They'll add any object as
an owner, a bot included.

68
00:04:26.947 --> 00:04:30.325
The reference they set is free
text, even though Learn's own

69
00:04:30.325 --> 00:04:33.773
guidance says to point at the
service management database.

70
00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:37.773
They say nothing about directory service
accounts, which is half the gate.

71
00:04:37.773 --> 00:04:41.860
And there's no orphan rule, no
disable and no claim window.

72
00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:46.977
So the verdict: keep Learn's query and its
owner requests as the model for the reads

73
00:04:46.977 --> 00:04:51.560
and for the one write that adds an owner,
but don't use the query as the check.

74
00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.950
It counts the wrong owners,
on the wrong object.

75
00:04:58.700 --> 00:04:59.762
Here's the check.

76
00:04:59.762 --> 00:05:02.363
It reads each owner and each nominee once,

77
00:05:02.363 --> 00:05:05.345
with a single call and a
single read permission,

78
00:05:05.345 --> 00:05:10.163
so it can tell a member from a guest and
an enabled account from a disabled one.

79
00:05:10.163 --> 00:05:11.924
Then it applies nine rules.

80
00:05:11.924 --> 00:05:16.527
The findings are the ones that block the
gate: no valid human owner, only one,

81
00:05:16.527 --> 00:05:21.356
a missing reference, a reference that
points at nothing or at a retired record,

82
00:05:21.356 --> 00:05:24.525
and a directory account
with no application owner.

83
00:05:24.525 --> 00:05:27.713
Guests, bots and disabled
owners are warnings.

84
00:05:27.713 --> 00:05:32.330
And anything that might be a person
isn't checked until someone confirms it.

85
00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.855
The orphan rule becomes a plan,
and nothing in the plan writes.

86
00:05:39.855 --> 00:05:44.730
Each orphan candidate and each
claim turns into exactly one action.

87
00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:48.699
Most are bookkeeping: waiting
for the officer's confirmation,

88
00:05:48.699 --> 00:05:52.201
a claim window still open, an
exception with an end date,

89
00:05:52.201 --> 00:05:55.392
or a retirement candidate
once the window closes.

90
00:05:55.392 --> 00:05:59.224
Only two actions ever write:
disabling a confirmed orphan,

91
00:05:59.224 --> 00:06:01.618
and adding an owner someone claimed.

92
00:06:01.618 --> 00:06:02.954
Here's the clever part.

93
00:06:02.954 --> 00:06:06.615
The same inputs always give
the same plan, byte for byte,

94
00:06:06.615 --> 00:06:09.543
so a reviewer doesn't
approve a description.

95
00:06:09.543 --> 00:06:11.250
They approve a hash.

96
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.588
This is the only code in the example that
can write, and it's built to refuse.

97
00:06:19.588 --> 00:06:23.620
It won't run unless the plan's hash
is the one the reviewer approved,

98
00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:27.801
the plan belongs to this tenant and
cloud, and it's less than a week old.

99
00:06:27.801 --> 00:06:30.162
It can only send a body that disables,

100
00:06:30.162 --> 00:06:33.075
or one that adds an owner,
and it never deletes.

101
00:06:33.075 --> 00:06:37.112
Before each write it looks again,
and skips if an owner has appeared.

102
00:06:37.112 --> 00:06:39.611
And it runs only as a
person, never an app,

103
00:06:39.611 --> 00:06:44.138
because the permission it needs could
let an app act as every other app.

104
00:06:44.138 --> 00:06:46.670
Disabling is never automatic.

105
00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:51.470
Who's involved?

106
00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.208
Developers build the code.

107
00:06:53.208 --> 00:06:58.177
The identity governance officer confirms
every orphan before anything is disabled,

108
00:06:58.177 --> 00:07:01.957
and the chief information security
officer owns the rule itself.

109
00:07:01.957 --> 00:07:05.780
Application owners and security
officers review the results,

110
00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:08.058
and a second person approves the plan.

111
00:07:08.058 --> 00:07:10.493
The inputs are deliberately plain files,

112
00:07:10.493 --> 00:07:13.572
each kept by the people who
actually know the answer:

113
00:07:13.572 --> 00:07:18.226
application owners for directory accounts,
the system inventory for records,

114
00:07:18.226 --> 00:07:23.095
the governance officer for confirmations,
and the offices that claim an identity.

115
00:07:23.095 --> 00:07:25.690
You don't need a tenant until Lab 2.

116
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.302
Lab 1 runs on a laptop, using the
register you built in Chapter 1's lab.

117
00:07:34.302 --> 00:07:38.108
First, send Learn's samples to
the fake, and read the test that

118
00:07:38.108 --> 00:07:42.302
drops the required header: the fake
refuses it, just as Graph would.

119
00:07:42.302 --> 00:07:45.803
Second, list what each task
needs, and notice the split:

120
00:07:45.803 --> 00:07:50.577
the check needs one read permission,
and only the write path asks for more.

121
00:07:50.577 --> 00:07:53.341
Third, run the check and build the plan.

122
00:07:53.341 --> 00:07:57.341
Twenty-two identities are checked,
seven are orphan candidates,

123
00:07:57.341 --> 00:08:01.040
and the plan has ten actions,
only three of which would write.

124
00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:04.510
Run it again, and the
plan's hash is the same.

125
00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:09.585
Now read what it found.

126
00:08:09.585 --> 00:08:11.810
Two apps have no valid owner at all.

127
00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:16.107
Four have only one, and the other
"owner" turns out to be a bot,

128
00:08:16.107 --> 00:08:18.210
a guest, or someone who's left.

129
00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:22.609
Several references are missing, free
text, or point at a retired record,

130
00:08:22.609 --> 00:08:26.098
and five directory accounts
have no application owner.

131
00:08:26.098 --> 00:08:27.983
The plan shows the rule at work:

132
00:08:27.983 --> 00:08:33.036
one account disabled last month and never
claimed is now a retirement candidate,

133
00:08:33.036 --> 00:08:37.185
another's window is still open, and
a claim naming a guest is refused.

134
00:08:37.185 --> 00:08:40.110
Then you dry-run the plan,
apply it to the fake,

135
00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:43.240
and watch an unapproved hash get refused.

136
00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:51.290
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant and
a test domain, never production.

137
00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:55.872
You create two deliberate orphans, an
app with no owners and a service account

138
00:08:55.872 --> 00:09:00.240
with no application owner, and run the
check with a single read permission.

139
00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.428
Both should show up.

140
00:09:01.428 --> 00:09:04.644
Then you do what the governance
officer would: confirm them,

141
00:09:04.644 --> 00:09:07.203
and have a second person
approve the plan's hash.

142
00:09:07.203 --> 00:09:11.215
Only then do you sign in as
a person, dry-run, and apply.

143
00:09:11.215 --> 00:09:14.176
The admin center shows
the app as deactivated,

144
00:09:14.176 --> 00:09:17.215
the account is disabled,
and you record the date.

145
00:09:17.215 --> 00:09:19.660
Then put everything back the way it was.

146
00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.198
Every danger here has a guard.

147
00:09:25.198 --> 00:09:29.934
The quiet one is counting the wrong owners,
a guest, a bot or someone who's left,

148
00:09:29.934 --> 00:09:33.372
so the gate passes on people
who can't answer for the app.

149
00:09:33.372 --> 00:09:37.884
The loud ones are a disable on a guess
and a delete, and the code answers both:

150
00:09:37.884 --> 00:09:42.322
nothing is disabled without the
officer's confirmation and a fresh look,

151
00:09:42.322 --> 00:09:44.097
and there is simply no delete.

152
00:09:44.097 --> 00:09:46.468
A plan changed after review won't run,

153
00:09:46.468 --> 00:09:49.786
and the write path can't
send anything but a disable.

154
00:09:49.786 --> 00:09:55.200
Notice, again, that each guard is a
rule the code enforces every time.

155
00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.300
Here's what done looks like.

156
00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:03.573
Every app has two owners who
are real, enabled people,

157
00:10:03.573 --> 00:10:06.313
or a finding with someone
assigned to fix it.

158
00:10:06.313 --> 00:10:08.652
Every reference points at a live record,

159
00:10:08.652 --> 00:10:12.837
and every directory account has an
application owner or is a candidate.

160
00:10:12.837 --> 00:10:16.875
No orphan was disabled without the
governance officer's confirmation.

161
00:10:16.875 --> 00:10:21.387
The plan's hash was approved by someone
other than the person who applied it,

162
00:10:21.387 --> 00:10:24.662
the write ran as a person, and
every disable has its date.

163
00:10:24.662 --> 00:10:26.019
With owners in place,

164
00:10:26.019 --> 00:10:30.250
Chapter 3 turns each account's
candidate target into a decision.

165
00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:31.420
Thanks for watching.
